---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout title="Sideboard Analysis - Replenish Guide">
    <PageHeader
        title="Sideboard Analysis"
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/decree.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Decree of Silence"
        imageCaption="Decree of Silence by Adam Rex"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="The Sculpting Method" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            I use this process before every tournament to finalize my 75. Start
            with a pool of 85-90 cards that you think could work in the deck,
            then carve away what doesn't fit the expected field.
        </p>
        <ol style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
            <li>
                <strong>Start Broad.</strong> 85–90 cards including variants and bullets.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Assess the Meta.</strong> Goblins heavy? Wraths. Blue heavy?
                Grids and creatures.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Prioritize Slots.</strong> Rank flex cards by standalone value
                and field impact.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Cut Strategically.</strong> Remove cards that don’t serve
                at least two matchups.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Finalize Sideboard.</strong> Transformative packages only
                if they address multiple matchups.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Main vs. Side.</strong> Placement based on expected frequency.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            <strong>The Creature Sideboard Package:</strong> Angel and Mage let you
            transform into a midrange deck after sideboarding. Your opponents face
            a bad choice: keep their enchantment hate and risk losing to creature
            beatdown, or cut it and risk losing to the combo. Either way their draws
            get worse.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Sideboarding Principles" titleTag="h2">
        <h3>Transformation Against Control</h3>
        <p>
            Against blue permission decks, you become a different deck. Frantic,
            Wave, Intuition, excess Swords all come out. Grid, Angels, Abeyance,
            Denial, fourth Mage go in. You're a midrange threat deck. If they
            sideboard to fight enchantments, they face Angels and Mages. If they
            respect the creatures, they face Replenish.
        </p>

        <h3>Keeping Combo Against Aggro</h3>
        <p>
            Against aggro the combo stays in. You swap interaction pieces. Seals
            come out when there aren't relevant targets.
        </p>

        <h3>Mage Exits Against Green</h3>
        <p>
            Against Rock, Ponza, and Terrageddon, Mages come out. Their
            creatures outclass a 2/2 and they don't depend on single spells Mage
            can name. Angels replace them.
        </p>

        <h3>Exalted Angel Is the Default In</h3>
        <p>
            Angel enters against every non-combo matchup: aggro, control, and
            midrange. It stays out against combo decks (Enchantress, the mirror,
            Elves) where diluting your own combo is too costly. If you’re unsure
            what to bring in against a non-combo deck, Angel is almost never
            wrong.
        </p>

        <h3>Frantic Search Is the Default Cut</h3>
        <p>
            Frantic exits primarily against control matchups where card
            disadvantage is punishing and discard heavy decks. Stays in against
            aggro and combo where velocity matters.
        </p>

        <h3>What to Trim Post-Board (General Rules)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Vs. Blue / Control:</strong> Cut <em>Intuition</em> and <em
                    >Frantic Search</em
                > (to avoid getting 2-for-1'd).
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Vs. Non-Creature Decks:</strong> Trim <em
                    >Swords to Plowshares</em
                >. Be careful though—many control decks bring in creatures like
                Meddling Mage or Exalted Angel from the sideboard, so leaving 1
                or 2 Swords is often correct.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong>Vs. Red Blast / Pyroblast (e.g., Sligh):</strong> Trim expensive
                blue spells (like <em>Fact or Fiction</em> or extra copies of <em
                    >Parallax Tide</em
                >) to limit the number of juicy four-mana targets vulnerable to
                a one-mana Blast.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Sideboard Card Analysis" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Defense Grid (3)"
            image="artifacts/defense_grid.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Comes in against every permission-heavy matchup. It sticks on
                the board, it comes down on a different turn than your threats,
                and it works with Decree cycling.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Exalted Angel (3)"
            image="creatures/exalted_angel.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                I board this in more than any other card. It goes in against
                aggro, control, and midrange. Basically every non-combo matchup.
                It stays out against Enchantress, the mirror, and Elves where
                you can't afford to dilute your combo. Against Sligh, even
                playing it face-down as a morph blocker that eats a Lightning
                Bolt is fine. Against control, it demands totally different
                answers than your enchantments. Against Terrageddon, if you're
                ahead on board with an Angel, they often can't cast Armageddon.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The Transformational Plan:</strong> Opponents often side out
                all their spot removal (like Swords to Plowshares) and load up on
                enchantment and graveyard hate (Aura of Silence, Tormod's Crypt, Naturalize).
                An early face-down Angel can easily steal games against UW, Enchantress,
                Rock, or Ponza Oath while they hold a hand full of dead anti-enchantment
                cards.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Abeyance (2)" image="instants/abeyance.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Protects your combo turn by shutting off their spells and
                non-mana abilities. Draws a card too. I bring it in against
                permission-heavy blue decks and in Opal-Wave combo matchups
                where disrupting one key turn is the difference.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Arcane Denial (1)"
            image="instants/arcane_denial.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Better than it looks through card advantage math. By itself,
                Arcane Denial is a natural +1 for your opponent (their spell is
                -1, you give them two cards which is +2, net +1 to them). But
                context determines whether that +1 is acceptable. If your
                opponent casts Armageddon and it resolves, that’s a +3 for
                them—destroying multiple lands. Giving them +1 with Arcane
                Denial is far better than letting them get +3. Another scenario:
                you cast Replenish, they counter it. Your Replenish is going to
                give you more than +1, so giving your opponent +1 to resolve a
                Replenish is ok. The similar logic applies to a face-down morph
                that gets countered: Arcane Denial your own morph to draw three
                cards (+2 for you) rather than Arcane Denying their counterspell
                to save the morph (which gives them +1 - a worse trade). Think
                of the card this way and it always makes sense.
            </p>
            <p>
                Arcane Denial enters against combo decks that rely on resolving
                a single powerful spell (Replenish mirror, PandeBurst, Devourer)
                and against control when protecting a critical spell.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Hydroblast (2)" image="instants/hydroblast.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Only for Sligh and Goblins. Countering Lackey on turn one is the
                single biggest sideboard play against Goblins.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 3rem;">
            Flex Slots (1 Mage / 1 Seal / 1 Swords / 1 Web)
        </h3>
        <p>
            Additional copies of maindeck cards. Fourth Mage for blue matchups.
            Fourth Seal for Enchantress. Fourth Swords for creature matchups.
            Extra Web for utility lands.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Other Sideboard Options" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis title="Orim's Chant" image="instants/orims_chant.webp">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                I had a hard time setting up Chant without Tide. Previously I
                could use Chant to force through a spell that would end the
                game. Going down on cards was fine because if the game ends, who
                cares. But without Tide, Decree is harder to set up for a full
                combo against control. I found that they could just let me
                Chant, let me resolve something, then dismantle it afterwards
                and I'd be down on cards. Defense Grid ended up being better
                than Chant or Abeyance against Landstill, Psychatog, and
                Stiflenought. Not by a huge amount, but enough.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Wrath of God" image="sorceries/wrath_of_god.webp">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Always in consideration. Extra removal against creature-heavy
                decks like Goblins and Elves. It's also relevant against
                opposing Meddling Mage if they're naming Swords, Wave, or both.
                I've had it in the sideboard before and could see it returning
                if creature decks pick up in the meta.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Sphere of Law"
            image="enchantments/sphere_of_law.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Has been in the sideboard in the past when Sligh was a heavy
                presence. Four mana to prevent two damage from every red source
                is a hard lock against most Sligh builds. Right now the red
                matchup is manageable enough without it, but if Sligh
                representation spikes, Sphere is the card you want.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Alter Reality" image="instants/alter_reality.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                A very spicy, highly specific piece of tech specifically to
                combat <code>Gloom</code> from Monoblack Moneyball variants. By casting
                this and changing the word "White" to "Black" on Gloom, their own
                spell taxes them instead of you. The flashback makes it extremely
                resilient to discard.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Disenchant" image="instants/disenchant.jpg">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>The Seal/Disenchant Split:</strong> With the rise of <code
                    >Aura of Silence</code
                > in the metagame, running exclusively <code
                    >Seal of Cleansing</code
                > in the sideboard has become a liability, because an opposing Aura
                prevents you from easily casting your Seals. Splitting your removal
                suite to include 1 or 2 <code>Disenchant</code> guarantees you have
                instant-speed answers that don't get shut down by Aura's static effect.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Playing Through Hate" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            A lot of beating hate comes down to knowing what your opponent is
            likely to have and not walking into it. You don't need a specific
            sideboard card for every hate piece. You just need to adjust how you
            sequence your game.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Withered Wretch"
            image="creatures/withered_wretch.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>(Black Aggro, Rock)</strong> Wretch is a real problem because
                it's repeatable and it's on a body that can attack you. If you know
                they have it, save your Swords for it. Don't use Swords on a random
                creature if you don’t have to and then lose because Wretch ate your
                graveyard. Prioritize removing it before you start committing enchantments
                to the yard.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Tormod's Crypt"
            image="artifacts/tormods_crypt.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The easy answer to this is an early Seal of Cleansing. But you
                can't always rely on having one, so knowing how to play through
                it matters.
            </p>
            <p>
                Against Crypt, the key is not putting yourself in a position
                where a single crypt beats you. Don't just blindly dump your
                hand into the graveyard and hope they don't have it. Use
                Intuition on your opponent's end step the turn before you
                Replenish. When activating Attunement, don't discard too many
                combo pieces if you're not ready to Replenish yet. If they
                already have Crypt in play, one line that works well: set up
                Attunement on the battlefield and hold your combo pieces in
                hand. Then with a minimal graveyard - just enough that they'd
                want to Crypt - cast Replenish. They use Crypt in response,
                wiping your graveyard. But Replenish is still on the stack.
                Still in response to your own Replenish, activate Attunement and
                dump a fresh graveyard. Replenish resolves and brings back
                everything you just discarded.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Phyrexian Furnace"
            image="artifacts/phyrexian_furnace.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Furnace is different because it only exiles one card at a time.
                Don't commit cards to the graveyard slowly — that just lets them
                pick off your best pieces one by one. If you're going to go for
                it, go for it all at once. Force them to make a choice about
                what one thing to remove. They can only get one card, and if the
                rest of your graveyard is good enough, Replenish still wins.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Tranquil Domain / Naturalize"
            image="instants/tranquil_domain.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Enchantment Removal:</strong> Decree of Silence is the easiest
                answer. If you Replenish with Decree, they need to fight through three
                counters before they can touch your board. The Parallax Wave protection
                trick also helps - with Opal-Wave, you can Wave your own enchantments
                to safety and they come back fresh. Another winning line is getting
                two Opalescences on the board, since you can use one to protect the
                other. Post-board against heavy enchantment removal, the creature
                package gives them a completely different problem to solve.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Meddling Mage"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Be aware of which decks can have this post-board. If you expect
                it, keep some amount of removal available. Parallax Wave handles
                it, Swords works, and Wrath of God if you have it. One Meddling
                Mage doesn't lock you out if you know it's coming.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Presence of the Master"
            image="enchantments/presence_of_the_master.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>The Replenish Bypass:</strong> While imposing on paper against an enchantment archetype, Presence of the Master strictly counters enchantment spells that are <em>played</em> (cast from your hand). <strong>It does not intercept enchantments entering the battlefield via Replenish!</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                However, a resolved Presence prevents you from casting manual combo pieces or hardcasting answers like Seal of Cleansing. To navigate around it, focus entirely on sculpting a decisive graveyard and winning directly off a single Replenish, or rely on instant-speed removal like Disenchant and non-enchantment proactive threats like Meddling Mage and Exalted Angel.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Aura of Silence"
            image="enchantments/aura_of_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Tax & Threat Combination:</strong> Frequently sideboarded in White Weenie, BW Control, and Enchantress mirrors. Because it increases the casting cost of your artifacts and enchantments by two generic mana, deploying early ramp or hardcasting combo engines becomes agonizingly slow. Worse still, sitting openly on the battlefield, it represents an immediate sacrifice trigger to destroy whatever key enchantment you do manage to resolve.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> This interaction underscores precisely why our sideboard engineers a split between Seal of Cleansing and Disenchant. Disenchant remains a clean two-mana instant entirely immune to Aura's static tax. In attrition matchups where Aura hits the table early, concentrate your mana on resolving Attunement and Frantic Search to load the graveyard, then cast a multi-enchantment Replenish; a single sacrifice trigger cannot dismantle a complete Opal-Wave-Decree lock entering simultaneously.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Scour"
            image="instants/scour.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Surgical Eradication:</strong> Primarily fielded by blue-white control sideboards utilizing Invasion-block removal suites, Scour threatens to destroy an enchantment on the battlefield and permanently exile all corresponding copies across your hand, library, and graveyard. A single resolved Scour targeting Opalescence or Parallax Wave can dismantle your foundational win condition for the rest of the match.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Never deploy an unprotected engine enchantment against open four-mana white suites post-sideboard without permission or a defensive plan. Most importantly, when initiating an Opal-Wave loop, maintain active fading counters on your Parallax Wave so you can instantly exile your own enchantments in response to Scour targeting them on the stack. If the target leaves the battlefield before resolution, Scour completely fizzles and zero copies are exiled.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Anarchy"
            image="sorceries/anarchy.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Red Sweeper & Color Hate:</strong> Aggressive red architectures (Sligh, RG Ponza, Goblins) consistently side in Anarchy to destroy white stabilization engines like Sphere of Law, Exalted Angel, Meddling Mage, Opalescence, and Parallax Wave in a single sweeping blow.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Anarchy is a four-mana sorcery that destroys rather than exiles. First, proactively throttle their mana development via Tsabo's Web and Rishadan Port to keep them off four operational mana, or name it defensively with Meddling Mage. Second, recognize that because Anarchy sends all destroyed enchantments directly to your graveyard, retaining a backup copy of Replenish allows you to instantly reanimate your entire wiped board on your subsequent turn, turning an apparent catastrophe into immediate game-ending tempo.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Gloom"
            image="matchups/moneyball/gloom.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Asymmetric Color Tax:</strong> Brought in by Black aggro, Suicide Black, and The Rock. By taxing all white spells and white enchantment activation costs by three generic mana, Gloom threatens to cripple Replenish, Swords to Plowshares, Opalescence, and Parallax Wave.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Fortunately, Gloom is strictly color-scoped to white. Your entire blue card-selection engine (Attunement, Frantic Search, Intuition, Deep Analysis) and blue permission suite (Mana Leak, Counterspell) function entirely unimpeded. Against heavily black fields, reserve early blue countermagic strictly for intercepting Gloom on the stack. If resolved, leverage sol lands (Ancient Tomb, City of Traitors) and artifact ramp to manually overcome the three-mana surcharge over extended games, or rely on blue bounce mechanics like Upheaval and cycling Decree of Silence.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Haunting Echoes"
            image="sorceries/haunting_echoes.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Graveyard & Library Extraction:</strong> The supreme terminal hate piece of Black-based control shells and Psychatog variants. Resolving a five-mana Haunting Echoes obliterates your accumulated graveyard while scouring your entire library of all namesake copies, completely disabling your combo architecture.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Because Haunting Echoes is a heavy five-mana sorcery, opponents are forced to tap down significantly during their own main phase to deploy it. Preserve cheap blue countermagic (Mana Leak, Counterspell, Arcane Denial) specifically for their fifth mana threshold, or proactively lock out the spell via Meddling Mage in Games 2 and 3. Avoid passively building an overflowing graveyard against five-mana black control pools without an immediate plan to execute Replenish on your own turn.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Reverent Silence"
            image="sorceries/reverent_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Free Enchantment Sweeper:</strong> Utilized by Elves and green aggro-midrange builds as zero-mana interaction by having you gain 6 life as an alternate casting cost. While sweeping your entire enchantment board for zero mana can ruin early stabilization pieces like Sphere of Law or Attunement, the dynamic is deeply advantageous if planned correctly.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> As a sorcery, opponents are restricted to casting Reverent Silence during their main phase. During preliminary setup rounds, gladly accept the 6-life cushion; every destroyed enchantment lands directly in your graveyard, actively advancing your Replenish setup at zero card cost to you. Once your Opal-Wave engine is fully assembled, simply activate Parallax Wave in response to stack self-protection triggers, bringing your entire board back safely once Reverent Silence resolves.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Winter Orb"
            image="artifacts/winter_orb.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>Mana Chokepoints:</strong> Featured heavily in Stasis, Merfolk, and aggressive tempo builds. Because Replenish operates around a critical three- and four-mana spell curve, restricted untap steps severely constrain your operational bandwidth and setup speed.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Prioritize removing Winter Orb immediately with Seal of Cleansing or instant-speed Disenchant. In game lines where Orb persists, sol lands like Ancient Tomb provide immense tactical leverage by generating two mana off a single untap step. Most importantly, remember that Frantic Search functions as an extraordinary burst untapper under Winter Orb: tap all available lands for mana before casting Search, then untap three lands upon resolution to suddenly generate a massive mana pool during a choked turn.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            collapsible={true}
            title="Cursed Totem"
            image="artifacts/cursed_totem.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                <strong>The Opalescence Lockout:</strong> A subtle and deceptively dangerous sideboard artifact. On an empty board or during standard setup phases, Cursed Totem has zero interaction with normal enchantments. However, <strong>the instant Opalescence enters the battlefield, all of your non-Aura enchantments transform into creatures.</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
                Under Opalescence and Cursed Totem simultaneously, Parallax Wave, Attunement, and Seal of Cleansing are strictly considered creatures with activated abilities—meaning Cursed Totem completely disables their activation loops! To circumvent this critical gotcha, always utilize Seal of Cleansing or instant-speed Disenchant to destroy Cursed Totem <em>before</em> deploying Opalescence onto the battlefield.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>
</Layout>
